2D ARRAYS

2D Arrays are the same as a standard array, with the key difference being that they store data in a 2D format rather than the linear
format used earlier.

Its syntax would be:

data-type [numerical value, numerical value] = { {value(s)}, {value(s)} };

EX:

  int[2,2] myEVILNumberList = { {4,20}, {81653, 17} };

Visualized it would be: |  4  |  20  |
                        | 81653 | 17 |

Much like the previous note, to modify a specific value in said array, one must specify 2 values. It helps if we define horizontal number lines as Rows and vertical lines as Columns, hereinthereafter refered to as such.
One value of a Row, one value of a Column, and any of its components are accesible. Lets say we want to modify the '17' value:

EX:
  myEVILNumberList[1,1] = 20;
  
You should be careful not to confuse the index markings, they still flow 0 to length-1!

